task: okay-mail-smtp
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-05
branch: feature/okay-mail-smtp
worktree: ../okay-wt-mail

why: a consumer's first BLOCKING request rather than an improving one.
  A one-time code to an email address is how a stranger proves the
  address is theirs; until they do, their profile is a draft. Their
  delivery today is `Identity.console` — the code is printed to the
  server log, marked development-only — so the service works and
  cannot have users. Everything they would do next is behind it,
  including the corpus of real messages that is the only lever left on
  every number I measured this week.

why in the library: sending mail is infrastructure, not a product, and
  it lands in a row this repository has already built twice — okay-pg
  is Postgres over the wire with its own SCRAM and TLS rather than a
  driver dependency, okay-http and okay-tls the same shape. SMTP is a
  smaller version of that job: a line-oriented text protocol over a
  socket, STARTTLS over the okay-tls that exists, AUTH PLAIN and LOGIN
  which are simpler than the SCRAM already written.

the shape they asked for, and I am not widening it:
  - SEND ONLY. Receiving is IMAP or POP, a different and much bigger
    module, and they argued against bundling it.
  - one message: envelope from, recipients, headers, a UTF-8 body. No
    MIME framework, no templates, no attachments until somebody has a
    case — "the feature arrives with its reader".
  - an EFFECT, so a caller wires a real server in main and a recorder
    in a test, the way `Transport` already works.
  - FAILURE AS DATA. A rejected recipient, a full mailbox and a
    refused relay are three different things and a caller answering a
    person must tell them apart; an exception collapses all three.

the design that makes it testable without a network: the WIRE is pure
  — given the server's lines, produce the client's lines and the
  outcome — and the socket is a thin shell over it. The pure half runs
  in the default gate; the loopback half binds a port and is therefore
  `Live`-tagged, per the standing policy.

